This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation.

Our research investigates the implications of the J/ψ particle decaying into invisible matter for light sterile neutrinos and sub-GeV dark matter. To understand these phenomena, we use low-energy effective field theories to characterize neutrino interactions and sub-GeV dark matter properties. Through an analysis of J/ψ decay into invisible particles, we determine the upper limits on effective field theory parameters, such as $\lambda_{
u\gamma}$ and $\lambda_{
u\delta}$, where $\lambda_{
u\gamma}$ governs the interaction between neutrinos and photons, and $\lambda_{
u\delta}$ controls neutrino interaction with a dark photon. We also establish the decay widths of a light sterile neutrino and a sub-GeV dark matter fermion. Our findings reveal that the present-day upper limit on the J/ψ decay into a light sterile neutrino is as tight as $10^{-4}$ GeV, when considering a dark momentum range of 200 MeV. Similarly, we determine the upper limit on the J/ψ decay into a sub-GeV dark matter fermion to be approximately $10^{-4}$ times its momentum in GeV. We then compare these results with existing experimental data on XENON1T's dark matter detection and find that a certain range of dark matter masses is inconconsistent with detected signals. Finally, we demonstrate that future experiments such as LHCb, with its capacity for higher momentum particles, has the potential to further restrict
